CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Management of Disaster Management Resources and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of Disaster Management Goods)
"Goods prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 2 (c) of Article 2 of the Act on Management of Disaster Management Resources (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following goods (excluding cash and securities):
1. Goods owned by, or held for use by, a management institution other than an administrative agency (meaning a management institution excluding central administrative agencies, local administrative agencies, and local governments; hereinafter referred to as a "management institution other than an administrative agency") that are necessary for its disaster management affairs;
2. Goods owned by, or held for use by, any of the following enterprises that are necessary in connection with its duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources:
(a) A national disaster management support enterprise designated pursuant to Article 9 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "national disaster management support enterprise");
(b) A regional disaster management support enterprise designated pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "regional disaster management support enterprise");
3. Goods owned by, or held for use by, a national disaster management logistics enterprise designated pursuant to Article 17 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "national disaster management logistics enterprise") that are necessary in connection with its duties concerning disaster management logistics;
4. Other goods owned or held for performance of a contract by a supplier that has entered into a unit price contract or a contract for services, etc. with the head of a management institution (hereinafter referred to as a "contracted supplier") and that are necessary for the disaster management affairs of the management institution.

Article 3 (Scope of Disaster Management Property)
(1) "Property prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 3 (c) of Article 2 of the Act means the following property that falls under paragraph (2):
1. Property owned or managed by a management institution other than an administrative agency that is necessary for its disaster management affairs;
2. Property owned or managed by national disaster management support enterprises and regional disaster management support enterprises that is necessary in connection with their duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources;
3. Property owned or managed by a national disaster management logistics enterprise that is necessary in connection with its duties concerning disaster management logistics;
4. Other property owned or managed by a contracted supplier for performance of a contract that is necessary for the disaster management affairs of a management institution.
(2) Disaster management property referred to in paragraph (1) shall be limited to the following property:
1. Real estate and its accessories;
2. Ships, floating piers, floating dry docks, and aircraft, and their accessories;
3. Railway vehicles, such as locomotives, electric cars, passenger coaches, freight cars, and motor railcars;
4. Other machinery and apparatus determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety in consultation with management institutions other than administrative agencies, national disaster management support enterprises, regional disaster management support enterprises, national disaster management logistics enterprises, and contracted suppliers.

Article 4 (Scope of Management Institutions)
(1) "Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 10 (b) of Article 2 of the Act means public institutions designated as management institutions by the head of a central administrative agency, taking into account their purposes of establishment, scope of functions, etc., from among the public institutions under that head's jurisdiction [excluding public institutions constituting disaster management responsible agencies under item (a) of that subparagraph (hereinafter referred to as "disaster management responsible agencies")], for systematic and efficient management of disaster management resources under that head's jurisdiction and disaster management at the national level.
(2) "Local public enterprises prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 10 (c) of Article 2 of the Act means local public enterprises designated as management institutions by the head of a local government, taking into account their purposes of establishment, scope of functions, etc., from among the local public enterprises under that head's jurisdiction (excluding local public enterprises constituting disaster management responsible agencies), for systematic and efficient management of disaster management resources under that head's jurisdiction and disaster management within the relevant jurisdiction.
(3) Where the head of a central administrative agency or the head of a local government designates a management institution pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2), the head shall, without delay, inform in writing the Minister of the Interior and Safety and the head of the institution operating (hereinafter referred to as the "information center operating institution") the National Information Center for Integrated Management of Disaster Management Resources designated pursuant to Article 47 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "National Integrated Resource Management Information Center") of the status of the designated management institution (hereinafter referred to as a "designated management institution").
(4) Details necessary for the management of designated management institutions shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

CHAPTER II SUPPLY CHAIN MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK AND DISASTER MANAGEMENT LOGISTICS FRAMEWORK

SECTION 1 SUPPLY CHAIN MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK

Article 5 (Joint Establishment of Supply Chain Management Framework)
(1) Where the head of a relevant central administrative agency intends to jointly establish with the Minister of the Interior and Safety a supply chain management framework for disaster management resources necessary for the disaster management affairs under that head's jurisdiction pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act, the head shall determine the following matters in advance and consult with the Minister of the Interior and Safety:
1. A plan for joint establishment of the supply chain management framework;
2. A plan for designation and management of national disaster management support enterprises;
3. A plan for linkage with the integrated disaster management resource management system under Article 46 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "integrated resource management system");
4. Other matters that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary for joint establishment of the supply chain management framework.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems it necessary for efficient establishment of the supply chain management framework, the Minister may request the head of a relevant central administrative agency to jointly establish a supply chain management framework under Article 6 (2) of the Act. In such cases, the head of the relevant central administrative agency shall examine the requested matters and conduct consultation under paragraph (1), unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.

Article 6 (Supply Chain Management Information)
"Information necessary for supply chain management prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the status of suppliers" in Article 7 (1) of the Act means the following information (hereinafter referred to as "supply chain management information"):
1. The supplier's trade name (meaning its name in the case of a corporation);
2. The location of the supplier's principal office or head office;
3. The supplier's main telephone number and website address;
4. The types and items of disaster management resources that the supplier sells, lends, or leases, or intends to sell, lend, or lease, to management institutions (including the countries of production and manufacturers of major raw and subsidiary materials in the case of disaster management goods);
5. The types of services that the supplier provides or intends to provide to management institutions and the types and items of disaster management resources used or utilized for those services;
6. Goods classification numbers and goods identification numbers under Article 9 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Management and Use of Information on the List of Goods (limited to disaster management goods that may be purchased using the national integrated electronic procurement system under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Use and Promotion of Electronic Procurement);
7. Other information, such as the scale of a supplier's production and sales, that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary for supply chain management.

Article 7 (Designation of National Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
(1) A supplier intending to be designated as a national disaster management support enterprise pursuant to Article 9 (1) of the Act shall satisfy all of the following criteria:
1. It shall be a supplier of disaster management resources necessary for disaster management at the national level;
2. It shall be a corporation with its head office in the Republic of Korea that conducts business nationwide and has facilities, equipment, and personnel capable of reliably managing and rapidly mobilizing disaster management resources;
3. It shall be capable of supplying disaster management resources necessary for urgent preparedness, response, and recovery activities for large-scale disasters under Article 14 (1) of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety (hereinafter referred to as the "Framework Act").
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to designate national disaster management support enterprises pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister may invite applications from suppliers of disaster management resources for which smooth supply is necessary.
(3) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall evaluate every five years whether a supplier designated as a national disaster management support enterprise is capable of properly performing duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources.
(4) Details necessary for designation of national disaster management support enterprises, invitations for applications, evaluations, and related matters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 8 (Orders for Preparatory Measures to National Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
(1) Pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management support enterprise to reinforce or expand facilities necessary for production, repair, processing, etc. of disaster management resources. In such cases, the Minister shall specifically notify the enterprise of the names of the facilities, scale, period, and other necessary matters.
(2) Pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management support enterprise conducting research on production, repair, processing, quality improvement, etc. of disaster management resources to develop technology (including production of prototypes; hereinafter the same shall apply), specifying the items, specifications, performance, period, and other necessary matters.
(3) Pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management support enterprise to establish a supply chain management system providing supply chain management information and link it with the integrated resource management system.

Article 9 (Grounds for Cancellation of Designation of National Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
"Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as closure of business, insolvency, or violation of this Act or an order under this Act" in Article 11 (1) of the Act means any of the following grounds:
1. Suspension or closure of business, or insolvency;
2. Bankruptcy proceedings being underway;
3. Neglect of the performance of duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources;
4. Being found, as a result of an evaluation under Article 7 (3), to have inadequate capability to perform duties;
5. Failure to comply with all or part of a mobilization order under Articles 51 and 52 of the Act or an order for preparatory measures under Article 8 of this Decree, or failure to pay an administrative fine under Article 65 of the Act.

Article 10 (Designation of Regional Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
(1) A supplier intending to be designated as a regional disaster management support enterprise pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Act shall satisfy all of the following criteria:
1. It shall be a supplier of disaster management resources necessary for disaster management within the relevant jurisdiction;
2. It shall be a corporation with its head office within the relevant jurisdiction (including a corporation without its head office within the relevant jurisdiction where unavoidable for disaster management within that jurisdiction) that conducts business within that jurisdiction and has facilities, equipment, and personnel capable of reliably managing and rapidly mobilizing disaster management resources;
3. It shall be capable of supplying disaster management resources necessary for urgent preparedness, response, and recovery activities for disasters that occur or are likely to occur within the relevant jurisdiction (meaning disasters under subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Framework Act; hereinafter the same shall apply).
(2) Where a Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") intends to designate regional disaster management support enterprises pursuant to paragraph (1), the Mayor/Do Governor may invite applications from suppliers of disaster management resources for which smooth supply is necessary. In such cases, the Mayor/Do Governor shall consult with the Minister of the Interior and Safety before inviting applications.
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor shall evaluate every five years whether a supplier designated as a regional disaster management support enterprise is capable of properly performing duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources.
(4) Details necessary for designation of regional disaster management support enterprises, invitations for applications, evaluations, and related matters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 11 (Orders for Preparatory Measures to Regional Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
(1) Pursuant to Article 13 (1) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor may order a regional disaster management support enterprise to reinforce or expand facilities necessary for production, repair, processing, etc. of disaster management resources. In such cases, the Mayor/Do Governor shall specifically state the names of the facilities, scale, period, and other necessary matters.
(2) Pursuant to Article 13 (1) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor may order a regional disaster management support enterprise conducting research on production, repair, processing, quality improvement, etc. of disaster management resources to develop technology, specifying the items, specifications, performance, period, and other necessary matters.
(3) Pursuant to Article 13 (1) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor may order a regional disaster management support enterprise to establish a supply chain management system providing supply chain management information and link it with the integrated resource management system.

Article 12 (Grounds for Cancellation of Designation of Regional Disaster Management Support Enterprises)
"Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as closure of business, insolvency, or violation of this Act or an order under this Act" in Article 14 (1) of the Act means any of the following grounds:
1. Suspension or closure of business, or insolvency;
2. Bankruptcy proceedings being underway;
3. Neglect of the performance of duties concerning management and mobilization of disaster management resources;
4. Being found, as a result of an evaluation under Article 10 (3), to have inadequate capability to perform duties;
5. Failure to comply with all or part of a mobilization order under Article 50 of the Act or an order for preparatory measures under Article 11 of this Decree, or failure to pay an administrative fine under Article 65 of the Act.

SECTION 2 DISASTER MANAGEMENT LOGISTICS FRAMEWORK

Article 13 (Designation of National Disaster Management Logistics Enterprises)
(1) A logistics enterprise intending to be designated as a national disaster management logistics enterprise pursuant to Article 17 (1) of the Act shall satisfy all of the following criteria:
1. It shall be a logistics enterprise engaging in comprehensive logistics services under Article 2 (1) 2 (d) of the Framework Act on Logistics Policies;
2. It shall operate a smart logistics center under subparagraph 5-4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Development and Operation of Logistics Facilities;
3. It shall be a corporation with its head office in the Republic of Korea that conducts logistics business nationwide and has facilities, equipment, and personnel capable of systematically storing and managing and rapidly transporting and mobilizing disaster management resources.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to designate national disaster management logistics enterprises pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister may invite applications from logistics enterprises where necessary for smooth logistics management.
(3) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall evaluate every five years whether a logistics enterprise designated as a national disaster management logistics enterprise is capable of properly performing duties concerning disaster management logistics.
(4) Details necessary for designation of national disaster management logistics enterprises, invitations for applications, evaluations, and related matters, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 14 (Orders for Preparatory Measures to National Disaster Management Logistics Enterprises)
(1) Pursuant to Article 18 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management logistics enterprise to expand logistics facilities and equipment necessary for efficient disaster management logistics activities. In such cases, the Minister shall specifically notify the enterprise of the matters according to the following classifications:
1. Logistics facilities: Names of the logistics facilities, scale, expansion period, and other necessary matters;
2. Equipment: Items, specifications, performance, expansion period, and other necessary matters.
(2) Pursuant to Article 18 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management logistics enterprise to link major logistics facilities and means of transport. In such cases, the Minister shall specifically notify the enterprise of the method of linking the logistics facilities and means of transport, linkage period, and other necessary matters.
(3) Pursuant to Article 18 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management logistics enterprise to pursue joint operation of disaster management logistics. In such cases, the Minister shall specifically notify the enterprise of the implementation method, implementation period, and other necessary matters.
(4) Pursuant to Article 18 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may order a national disaster management logistics enterprise to establish a disaster management logistics system for receipt and dispatch, inventory management, transport management, etc. of disaster management resources and link it with the integrated resource management system.

Article 15 (Grounds for Cancellation of Designation of National Disaster Management Logistics Enterprises)
"Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as closure of business, insolvency, or violation of this Act or an order under this Act" in Article 19 (1) of the Act means any of the following grounds:
1. Suspension or closure of business, or insolvency;
2. Bankruptcy proceedings being underway;
3. Neglect of the performance of duties concerning disaster management logistics;
4. Being found, as a result of an evaluation under Article 13 (3), to have inadequate capability to perform duties;
5. Failure to comply with all or part of a mobilization order under Articles 51 and 52 of the Act or an order for preparatory measures under Article 14 of this Decree, or failure to pay an administrative fine under Article 65 of the Act.

CHAPTER III PERSONNEL FOR MANAGEMENT OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT RESOURCES

Article 16 (Delegation of Affairs concerning Management of Disaster Management Resources)
(1) Where the head of a management institution intends to delegate affairs concerning management of disaster management resources to at least two public officials or employees belonging to the institution (hereinafter referred to as "public officials, etc.") pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act, the head shall designate one of them as the chief resource management officer and require that officer to oversee and coordinate affairs concerning management of disaster management resources.
(2) Where the head of a management institution intends to delegate affairs concerning management of disaster management resources under that head's jurisdiction to public officials, etc. belonging to another management institution pursuant to Article 20 (1) of the Act, the head shall consult in advance with the head of that management institution.

Article 17 (Delegation of Affairs concerning Receipt, Issue, Storage, Use, and Utilization of Disaster Management Resources)
(1) Where a resource management officer (including public officials, etc. assigned responsibility for part of that officer's affairs pursuant to Article 23 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) delegates affairs concerning receipt, issue, and storage of disaster management resources (excluding affairs concerning orders for receipt and issue) to public officials, etc. of the management institution to which the officer belongs pursuant to Article 21 (1) of the Act, the officer shall comply with what is determined by the head of the management institution.
(2) Where a resource management officer has public officials, etc. of the management institution to which the officer belongs use or utilize disaster management resources according to the purposes and uses of disaster management affairs, or delegates to them affairs concerning management of disaster management resources in use or being utilized pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Act, the officer shall comply with what is determined by the head of the management institution.

Article 18 (Division of Duties and Acting Arrangements in Management Institutions)
(1) The head of a management institution may appoint public officials, etc. falling under any of the following subparagraphs to take responsibility for part of the affairs of a resource management officer pursuant to Article 23 (1) of the Act:
1. Public officials, etc. assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a resource management officer as determined by the head of the management institution;
2. Public officials assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a goods management officer pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Goods Management Act;
3. Public officials assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a goods management officer pursuant to Article 55 (1) of the Public Property and Goods Management Act.
(2) A resource management officer may appoint public officials, etc. falling under any of the following subparagraphs to take responsibility for part of the affairs of a resource receipt and issue officer pursuant to Article 23 (1) of the Act:
1. Public officials, etc. assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a resource receipt and issue officer as determined by the head of the management institution;
2. Public officials assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a public official in charge of goods receipt and issue pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Goods Management Act;
3. Public officials assigned responsibility for part of the affairs of a public official in charge of goods receipt and issue pursuant to Article 55 (1) of the Public Property and Goods Management Act.
(3) The head of a management institution may designate public officials, etc. falling under any of the following subparagraphs to act for a resource management officer in performing that officer's affairs pursuant to Article 23 (2) of the Act:
1. Public officials, etc. who act for a resource management officer in performing that officer's affairs as determined by the head of the management institution;
2. Public officials who act for a goods management officer in performing that officer's affairs pursuant to Article 12 (2) of the Goods Management Act;
3. Public officials who act for a goods management officer in performing that officer's affairs pursuant to Article 55 (2) of the Public Property and Goods Management Act.
(4) A resource management officer may designate public officials, etc. falling under any of the following subparagraphs to act for a resource receipt and issue officer or a resource utilization officer in performing that officer's affairs pursuant to Article 23 (2) of the Act:
1. Public officials, etc. who act for a resource receipt and issue officer or a resource utilization officer in performing that officer's affairs as determined by the head of the management institution;
2. Public officials who act for a public official in charge of goods receipt and issue or a goods utilization officer in performing that official's or officer's affairs pursuant to Article 12 (2) of the Goods Management Act;
3. Public officials who act for a public official in charge of goods receipt and issue or a goods utilization officer in performing that official's or officer's affairs pursuant to Article 55 (2) of the Public Property and Goods Management Act.

CHAPTER IV MANAGEMENT OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT GOODS

SECTION 1 GENERAL RULES ON DISASTER MANAGEMENT GOODS

Article 19 (Classification of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) The head of a management institution shall classify and manage disaster management goods under that head's jurisdiction pursuant to Article 25 (1) of the Act in accordance with the classification criteria for disaster management goods determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety, taking into account the following matters:
1. Functions under the subparagraphs of Article 43-5 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety;
2. Types of disasters and accidents prescribed in attached Table 1-3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety;
3. The nature of disaster management goods according to the following classifications:
(a) Reusable disaster management goods: Disaster management goods that can be stored for at least one year and can be used continuously;
(b) Disposable disaster management goods: Disaster management goods that can be stored for at least one year and can be used only once;
(c) Consumable disaster management goods: Goods that cannot be stored for at least one year;
4. Other matters that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary for classification of disaster management goods.
(2) The head of a management institution shall assign catalog numbers to and manage disaster management goods under that head's jurisdiction in accordance with the standards determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as the "goods catalog standards").
(3) Where it is difficult to assign a catalog number to disaster management goods in accordance with the goods catalog standards, the head of a management institution shall request the Minister of the Interior and Safety to designate a catalog number and shall assign the catalog number according to the result. In such cases, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may have the information center operating institution examine the designation of the catalog number.

Article 20 (Standardization of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where the head of a management institution establishes standards for disaster management goods used only by that management institution and its affiliated institutions and subordinate institutions, organizations, and corporations pursuant to Article 26 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "institutional standards"), the head shall submit the institutional standards to the Minister of the Interior and Safety without delay.
(2) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety establishes standards for disaster management goods commonly used by management institutions pursuant to Article 26 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "national standards"), the Minister shall publicly notify the content of the national standards, their enforcement date, and other necessary matters.
(3) Where any of the following standards or criteria exist for disaster management goods, the head of a management institution and the Minister of the Interior and Safety may select those standards or criteria as institutional standards and national standards, or use those standards or criteria as they are or with supplementation of only necessary matters without establishing separate institutional standards and national standards:
1. Standards for major goods under Article 5 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Goods Management Act;
2. Industrial standards under Article 11 of the Industrial Standardization Act;
3. Safety standards, inspection standards, or technical standards under other statutes or regulations;
4. Other standards or criteria used for the relevant disaster management goods.
(4) The head of a management institution and the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall review every three years whether institutional standards and national standards need to be amended or abolished; provided, where they find that application of institutional standards and national standards is difficult or impossible due to technological advances or similar circumstances, they may amend or abolish them from time to time.
(5) Details necessary for standardization of disaster management goods, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

SECTION 2 STOCKPILING AND MANAGEMENT OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT GOODS

Article 21 (Formulation of Stockpile Management Plans for Disaster Management Goods)
(1) The head of a management institution shall formulate a stockpile management plan for disaster management goods under that head's jurisdiction for the following year (hereinafter referred to as a "stockpile management plan") by September 30 each year pursuant to the former part, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 28 (1) of the Act, and shall submit it (including submission through the integrated resource management system; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article and Article 22) to the head of the institution according to the classifications in the subparagraphs of that paragraph (hereinafter referred to as the "head of the plan compilation institution").
(2) To support the heads of management institutions in systematically formulating stockpile management plans for the following year, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may prepare guidelines for the formulation of stockpile management plans and notify the heads of management institutions thereof by May 31 each year.
(3) Where the head of the plan compilation institution examines a stockpile management plan submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and deems supplementation necessary, the head may require the head of the relevant management institution to supplement it. In such cases, the head of the management institution requested to supplement the plan shall supplement and resubmit the stockpile management plan within 15 days from the date of receiving the request.
(4) The heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors shall compile the stockpile management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and the latter part of paragraph (3) and submit them to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by November 30 each year pursuant to the latter part, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 28 (1) of the Act.
(5) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety examines stockpile management plans submitted by the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to paragraph (4) and deems supplementation necessary, the Minister may require the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors to supplement them. In such cases, the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors requested to supplement the plans shall, after hearing the opinions of the heads of the relevant management institutions, supplement and resubmit the stockpile management plans within 30 days from the date of receiving the request.

Article 22 (Changes to Stockpile Management Plans)
(1) Where the head of a management institution changes a stockpile management plan submitted to the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 21 for an unavoidable reason, such as a budget amendment or transfer of budget appropriations, the head shall submit the changed stockpile management plan (including the reasons for the change) to the Minister of the Interior and Safety through the head of the plan compilation institution without delay.
(2) Article 21 (3) and (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to supplementation of stockpile management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1).

Article 23 (Stockpile Management Standards for Disaster Management Goods)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall establish stockpile management standards for major disaster management goods under the part, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 29 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "stockpile management standards"), taking into account the following matters:
1. Matters necessary for setting standard quantities, such as the functions, workload, and number of personnel managed by management institutions; the frequency and scale of disasters managed by each management institution; the number of disaster victims; and other criteria for setting standard quantities prescribed by other statutes or regulations;
2. Matters necessary for setting useful lives, such as average periods of use, durability periods prescribed by other statutes or regulations, durability periods determined by the Korea Real Estate Board under the Korea Real Estate Board Act, and durability periods determined by suppliers;
3. Matters necessary for inventory management, such as frequency of use and the need to maintain inventory.
(2) Where the head of a management institution independently establishes stockpile management standards pursuant to the former part of Article 29 (2) of the Act, the head shall take into account the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) of this Article.

Article 24 (Adjustment of Stockpile Management Standards)
(1) Where the head of a management institution deems it necessary to adjust the stockpile management standards notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 29 (1) of the Act, the head shall consult in advance with the head of the competent central administrative agency or the Mayor/Do Governor, prepare a proposal for adjustment of the stockpile management standards and a statement of reasons, and submit them to the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(2) A resource management officer shall regularly examine matters requiring adjustment of the stockpile management standards each year and report them to the head of the management institution to which the officer belongs.
(3) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety adjusts stockpile management standards pursuant to Article 29 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall examine the following matters and reflect the results:
1. Results of inventory surveys or special inventory surveys under Article 30 of the Act;
2. Results of inspections under Article 59 of the Act;
3. Proposals for adjustment of stockpile management standards and statements of reasons submitted by the heads of management institutions pursuant to paragraph (1).

Article 25 (Institution for Research and Development of Stockpile Management Standards)
"Affiliated institution prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 29 (5) of the Act means the National Disaster Management Research Institute.

Article 26 (Inventory Surveys of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall establish guidelines concerning inventory surveys regularly conducted by the heads of management institutions pursuant to Article 30 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "regular inventory surveys") (hereinafter referred to as "inventory survey guidelines") and notify the heads of management institutions thereof by September 30 each year.
(2) The head of a management institution shall conduct a regular inventory survey as of December 31 each year in accordance with the inventory survey guidelines, prepare a regular inventory survey report, and submit it (including submission through the integrated resource management system; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) to the head of the plan compilation institution by the end of February of the following year.
(3) The head of the plan compilation institution shall examine a regular inventory survey report submitted pursuant to paragraph (2) and, where supplementation is necessary, require the head of the relevant management institution to supplement it without delay. In such cases, the head of the management institution requested to supplement the report shall supplement and resubmit the regular inventory survey report by March 15 of the relevant year.
(4) Where the head of a management institution deems it necessary to check the status of management of disaster management goods under that head's jurisdiction, such as where the resource management officer or resource receipt and issue officer is replaced, the head may conduct an inventory survey from time to time pursuant to Article 30 (1) of the Act.
(5) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety or a Mayor/Do Governor deems it necessary to check the status of management of disaster management goods due to organizational changes or similar circumstances, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor may conduct a special inventory survey of the disaster management goods of management institutions pursuant to Article 30 (2) of the Act.
(6) Details necessary for inventory surveys, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 27 (Inventory Adjustments of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where the head of a management institution makes an inventory adjustment pursuant to Article 30 (4) of the Act, the head shall ascertain the causes of the increase or decrease, clearly state the following matters, adjust the current quantities and values in the standard form under Article 28 (3) of the Act, and enter them in the integrated resource management system:
1. Catalog numbers, names, and items of the disaster management goods subject to inventory adjustment;
2. Quantities and values in the standard form as of the date of the inventory survey;
3. Quantities and values of increases or decreases discovered as a result of the inventory survey;
4. Quantities and values in the standard form after inventory adjustment;
5. Reasons for the inventory adjustment.
(2) Details necessary for inventory adjustments under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 28 (Transfer of Management of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where a resource management officer transfers disaster management goods under the officer's jurisdiction to the jurisdiction of another resource management officer pursuant to Article 30 (5) of the Act, the resource management officers shall reach mutual agreement, clearly state the content of the agreement, and obtain prior approval from the head of the management institution.
(2) Where a resource management officer has received a mobilization order under Articles 50 through 52 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "mobilization order") and it is necessary, the officer may transfer disaster management goods under the officer's jurisdiction to the jurisdiction of another resource management officer without prior approval from the head of the management institution; provided, the officer shall obtain approval from the head of the management institution within 30 days after the mobilization ends.
(3) Where a resource management officer transfers disaster management goods under the officer's jurisdiction to the jurisdiction of a resource management officer for another account, the transfer shall be for consideration; provided, where the transfer is for the purpose of having the goods used for official or public use in another account, it may be made without consideration.
(4) Where goods are transferred for consideration to the jurisdiction of a resource management officer for another account pursuant to the main clause of paragraph (3), the price shall be the book price of the relevant disaster management goods; provided, where transfer at the book price is difficult, the transfer may be made at the market price.
(5) Details necessary for transfer of management of disaster management goods, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 29 (Maintenance of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) A resource management officer shall select major disaster management goods requiring maintenance pursuant to Article 31 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "disaster management goods subject to maintenance") and shall formulate an annual maintenance plan for the disaster management goods subject to maintenance, including the timing and method of maintenance and a plan for the supply and demand of spare parts. In such cases, the plan for the supply and demand of spare parts may be formulated as part of the stockpile management plan.
(2) Where a resource management officer maintains disaster management goods subject to maintenance in accordance with a maintenance plan under paragraph (1), the officer shall establish maintenance standards in advance. In such cases, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may establish guidelines on maintenance standards.
(3) Where necessary for systematic and efficient maintenance of disaster management goods subject to maintenance, a resource management officer may request a supplier, a specialized maintenance business, etc. to maintain all or part of the disaster management goods subject to maintenance.
(4) Where a resource management officer (including a resource utilization officer) has performed maintenance on disaster management goods subject to maintenance, the officer shall, without delay, enter in the integrated resource management system the matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, such as the names of the relevant disaster management goods subject to maintenance and the maintenance period.

SECTION 3 ACQUISITION, STORAGE, AND USE OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT GOODS

Article 30 (Acquisition of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) When requesting public officials, etc. in charge of contracts to take measures necessary for acquisition of disaster management goods pursuant to Article 32 (1) of the Act, a resource management officer shall clearly specify the catalog numbers, names, specifications, and quantities of the disaster management goods to be acquired and the time when the disaster management goods are needed.
(2) Where public officials, etc. in charge of contracts are unable to take measures requested by a resource management officer pursuant to paragraph (1) due to budgetary circumstances or other unavoidable reasons, they shall notify the resource management officer of that fact.
(3) When taking measures necessary for acquisition of disaster management goods at the request of a resource management officer under paragraph (1), public officials, etc. in charge of contracts shall comply with the following criteria:
1. Where the disaster management goods to be acquired are procurement commodities under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Government Procurement Act, they shall request the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service to purchase them;
2. Where the purpose of acquiring disaster management goods can be achieved with the following goods, those goods shall be acquired preferentially:
(a) Disaster management goods of a national disaster management support enterprise or a regional disaster management support enterprise that have been standardized pursuant to Article 20;
(b) Disaster safety products certified pursuant to Article 16 of the Disaster Safety Industry Promotion Act.
(4) Where public officials, etc. in charge of contracts who receive a request under the former part of Article 32 (2) of the Act take measures necessary for acquisition of disaster management goods pursuant to paragraph (3) of that Article, they shall, without delay, acquire by a negotiated contract the disaster management goods of a national disaster management support enterprise or a regional disaster management support enterprise requested by the resource management officer.

Article 31 (Stockpile Facilities for Disaster Management Goods)
(1) "Stockpile facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 33 (1) of the Act means logistics facilities under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Development and Operation of Logistics Facilities and logistics warehouses under subparagraph 5-2 of that Article that are stockpile facilities meeting the criteria under paragraph (2).
(2) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall determine and publicly notify criteria for the structure, scale, installation, devices, etc. required of stockpile facilities under paragraph (1), taking into account the following matters:
1. Types and items of disaster management goods;
2. The nature of disaster management goods and ease of storage;
3. The need for integrated management of disaster management goods;
4. Other conditions for use and utilization of disaster management goods.

Article 32 (Storage of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where a resource management officer intends to store disaster management goods in stockpile facilities, etc. of another management institution pursuant to the proviso of Article 33 (1) of the Act, the officer shall clearly state the following matters and request public officials, etc. in charge of contracts to take measures necessary for the storage:
1. Reasons why storage is necessary;
2. Catalog numbers, names, items, and quantities of the disaster management goods requiring storage;
3. Storage period;
4. Storage location and stockpile facilities;
5. Incidental conditions of storage.
(2) Where public officials, etc. in charge of contracts are unable to take measures requested by a resource management officer pursuant to paragraph (1) due to budgetary circumstances or other unavoidable circumstances, they shall notify the resource management officer of that fact.

Article 33 (Orders for Receipt and Issue of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) When ordering receipt or issue of disaster management goods pursuant to Article 33 (2) of the Act, a resource management officer shall clearly specify the following matters:
1. Catalog numbers, names, items, and quantities of the disaster management goods to be received or issued;
2. Timing of receipt or issue;
3. The person delivering to the resource receipt and issue officer the disaster management goods to be received or issued and the person taking delivery of them from the resource receipt and issue officer.
(2) Where a mobilization order is issued, an order for receipt and issue by the resource management officer under Article 33 (2) of the Act shall be deemed to have been issued. In such cases, the resource management officer shall enter the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) in the integrated resource management system within 30 days after the mobilization ends.

Article 34 (Repair or Modification of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) When requesting measures necessary for repair or modification of disaster management goods pursuant to Article 33 (5) of the Act, a resource management officer shall clearly specify the following matters:
1. Catalog numbers, names, items, and quantities of the disaster management goods requiring repair or modification;
2. Timing of repair or modification;
3. Details of repair or modification;
4. Conditions to be attached to repair or modification.
(2) Where public officials, etc. in charge of contracts are unable to take measures requested by a resource management officer pursuant to paragraph (1) due to budgetary circumstances or other unavoidable circumstances, they shall notify the resource management officer of that fact.

Article 35 (Use of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) When requesting an order for receipt and issue of disaster management goods pursuant to the proviso of Article 34 (1) of the Act, a resource utilization officer shall clearly specify the catalog numbers, names, quantities, and uses of the disaster management goods for which the request is made.
(2) A resource utilization officer shall clearly specify the catalog numbers, names, quantities, uses, and users of disaster management goods when permitting public officials, etc. to use them.
(3) Where a mobilization order is issued, a resource utilization officer may immediately permit public officials, etc. to use disaster management goods, notwithstanding paragraph (2). In such cases, the resource utilization officer shall enter the catalog numbers, etc. of the disaster management goods under paragraph (2) in the integrated resource management system within 30 days after the mobilization ends.

SECTION 4 DISPOSAL OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT GOODS

Article 36 (Exchange of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where the head of a management institution intends to exchange disaster management goods pursuant to Article 35 (1) of the Act, the head shall clearly state the reasons for the exchange, prices and uses of the disaster management goods to be exchanged, the other party to the exchange, and other relevant matters. In such cases, where the prices of the disaster management goods to be exchanged differ, the difference shall be calculated and settled in money without delay after the exchange.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where an exchange is made for urgent disaster management, prices and differences may be ascertained and settled afterward.

Article 37 (Declaration of Disaster Management Goods as Unneeded and Their Disposal)
(1) "Disaster management goods prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 36 (1) of the Act means disaster management goods that have an acquisition unit price of at least five million won and are capable of being utilized.
(2) "Disaster management goods prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 36 (2) of the Act means disaster management goods that have an acquisition unit price of at least five million won.

Article 38 (Special Cases concerning Sale of Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where disaster management goods intended for sale (meaning disaster management goods intended for sale under the proviso of Article 37 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) or unneeded goods (meaning disaster management goods determined to be unneeded pursuant to Article 36 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) are sold through general competitive bidding, and there is at least one valid bid at or above the estimated price, the highest bidder shall be the successful bidder.
(2) Disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods falling under any of the following subparagraphs may be sold by auction or negotiated contract pursuant to Article 37 (2) of the Act:
1. Where disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods that have failed to be sold through general competitive bidding on at least two occasions are sold;
2. Where disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods with a disposal unit price not exceeding 100,000 won and a total disposal price not exceeding five million won are sold;
3. Where disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods with a disposal unit price not exceeding five million won and a total disposal price not exceeding ten million won are sold to persons prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety;
4. Where disaster management goods mobilized pursuant to Articles 49 through 52 of the Act are sold to management institutions prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
(3) Where disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods are sold pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2), the estimated price shall be an actual transaction price or the appraised value determined upon requesting an appraisal by an appraisal corporation, etc. under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Appraisal and Certified Appraisers (hereinafter referred to as an "appraisal corporation, etc."); provided, for the following disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods, the estimated price may be determined by obtaining a quotation from the prospective contracting party or a third party:
1. Disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods with an acquisition unit price not exceeding 100,000 won;
2. Disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods for which the fee of an appraisal corporation, etc. is expected to exceed the estimated price or appraisal by an appraisal corporation, etc. is difficult.
(4) An estimated price determined pursuant to paragraph (3) shall not be disclosed to a person intending to purchase the relevant disaster management goods intended for sale or unneeded goods; provided, it may be disclosed where disclosure of the estimated price is deemed likely to facilitate the sale.

Article 39 (Management, Repair, and Disposal of Reusable Goods)
(1) Where the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service deems it necessary for efficient management of unneeded goods capable of being utilized for which management has been transferred to the Administrator without consideration pursuant to Article 38 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "reusable goods"), the Administrator may select a person deemed capable from among persons conducting businesses capable of putting reusable goods to use again and enter into a contract for management of the reusable goods.
(2) A contract under paragraph (1) may be concluded by negotiated contract.
(3) The consideration paid to a person selected pursuant to paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "reuse business operator") shall be determined taking into account the costs incurred for collection, transport, repair, disposal, etc. of the relevant reusable goods, but shall not exceed three-fourths of the proceeds from disposal of the reusable goods.
(4) Where a reuse business operator disposes of reusable goods, the operator shall follow the method designated by the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service, and the disposal price shall be determined by the reuse business operator taking into account transaction prices of disaster management goods identical or similar to the reusable goods.
(5) Where the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service deems it necessary for efficient disposal of reusable goods, the Administrator may require a reuse business operator to obtain prior approval before determining the disposal price of reusable goods under paragraph (4).

Article 40 (Gratuitous Transfer of Unneeded Goods)
(1) Where the head of a management institution transfers unneeded goods without consideration to persons referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 39 (1) of the Act pursuant to that paragraph, the head shall clearly state the following matters:
1. Catalog numbers, names, items, and quantities of the disaster management goods;
2. History of use of the disaster management goods;
3. Condition of the disaster management goods;
4. Reasons for gratuitous transfer.
(2) "Educational and research institutions and nonprofit corporations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 39 (1) 2 of the Act means corporations falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. An institution or corporation for which the State or a local government contributes or subsidizes at least one-half of the annual costs of the institution's or corporation's business;
2. An institution or corporation in which a management institution invests or contributes at least one-half of its capital or property;
3. A nonprofit corporation performing functions entrusted by the State or a local government or conducting a public interest project.
(3) "Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 39 (1) 3 of the Act means persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. A recipient under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the National Basic Living Security Act;
2. A disaster victim under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Disaster Relief Act or a temporarily evacuated person under subparagraph 2 of that Article;
3. A person eligible for support under Article 5 or 5-2 of the Single-Parent Family Support Act.

Article 41 (Handling of Lost or Damaged Disaster Management Goods)
(1) Where the head of a management institution issues an order for reimbursement pursuant to Article 40 (1) of the Act, the head shall do so by the following methods:
1. Reimbursement shall be made in cash, but where requiring cash reimbursement would be disadvantageous to the management institution, reimbursement shall be made in kind;
2. The amount of reimbursement shall be converted into the market value at the time the loss or damage of the disaster management goods occurred; provided, where the time of occurrence is unclear, it shall be converted into the market value at the time the loss or damage was discovered.
(2) Where the head of a management institution issues an order for reimbursement under paragraph (1), the head shall inform the Chairperson of the Board of Audit and Inspection of the result in writing without delay; provided, the head of a management institution that is not subject to audit or inspection by the Board of Audit and Inspection under the Board of Audit and Inspection Act (hereinafter referred to as a "management institution not subject to audit") shall inform the head of the competent central administrative agency or local government of the result in writing.
(3) Where public officials, etc. engaged in affairs concerning management of disaster management goods who have received an order for reimbursement under Article 40 (1) of the Act have grounds for exemption from liability, they may send to the Board of Audit and Inspection (meaning the competent central administrative agency or local government in the case of public officials, etc. belonging to a management institution not subject to audit; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) documents clearly stating those grounds, together with documents substantiating them, and request an adjudication.
(4) Documents sent to the Board of Audit and Inspection pursuant to paragraph (3) shall pass through the head of the management institution to which the relevant public officials, etc. belong.

CHAPTER V MANAGEMENT OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT PROPERTY AND DISASTER MANAGEMENT PERSONNEL

Article 42 (Formulation of Management Plans for Disaster Management Property)
(1) The head of a management institution shall formulate a management plan for disaster management property under that head's jurisdiction for the following year (hereinafter referred to as a "property management plan") by September 30 each year pursuant to the former part of Article 41 (1) of the Act and shall submit it (including submission through the integrated resource management system; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article and Article 43) to the head of the plan compilation institution.
(2) To support the heads of management institutions in systematically formulating property management plans under their jurisdiction for the following year, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may prepare guidelines for formulation of property management plans and notify the heads of management institutions thereof by May 31 each year.
(3) Where the head of the plan compilation institution examines a property management plan submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and deems supplementation necessary, the head may require the head of the relevant management institution to supplement it. In such cases, the head of the management institution requested to supplement the plan shall supplement and resubmit the property management plan within 15 days from the date of receiving the request.
(4) The heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors shall compile the property management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and the latter part of paragraph (3) of this Article and submit them to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by November 30 each year pursuant to the latter part of Article 41 (1) of the Act.
(5) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety examines property management plans submitted by the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to paragraph (4) and deems supplementation necessary, the Minister may require the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors to supplement them. In such cases, the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors requested to supplement the plans shall, after hearing the opinions of the heads of the relevant management institutions, supplement and resubmit the property management plans within 30 days from the date of receiving the request.

Article 43 (Changes to Property Management Plans)
(1) Where the head of a management institution changes a property management plan submitted to the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 42 for an unavoidable reason, such as disposal of disaster management property subject to management, the head shall submit the changed property management plan (including the reasons for the change) to the Minister of the Interior and Safety through the head of the plan compilation institution without delay.
(2) Article 42 (3) and (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to supplementation of property management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1).

Article 44 (Matters to Be Recorded and Maintained concerning Disaster Management Property)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as types and history of use" in Article 41 (3) of the Act means the following matters:
1. Types and uses of disaster management property;
2. Owners of disaster management property;
3. The status of resource management officers, resource receipt and issue officers, and resource utilization officers for disaster management property;
4. The location or storage place of disaster management property;
5. The history of use or utilization of disaster management property [including the history of mobilization under Articles 49 through 52 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "mobilization history")];
6. Other matters necessary for management of disaster management property prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
(2) The head of a management institution shall assign catalog numbers to and manage disaster management property under that head's jurisdiction in accordance with the standards determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as the "property catalog standards").
(3) Article 19 (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to designation and assignment of catalog numbers for disaster management property to which it is difficult to assign catalog numbers in accordance with the property catalog standards.

Article 45 (Formulation of Management Plans for Disaster Management Personnel)
(1) The head of a management institution shall formulate a management plan for disaster management personnel under that head's jurisdiction for the following year (hereinafter referred to as a "personnel management plan") by September 30 each year pursuant to the former part of Article 43 (1) of the Act and shall submit it (including submission through the integrated resource management system; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article and Article 46) to the head of the plan compilation institution.
(2) To support the heads of management institutions in systematically formulating personnel management plans under their jurisdiction for the following year, the Minister of the Interior and Safety may prepare guidelines for formulation of personnel management plans and notify the heads of management institutions thereof by May 31 each year.
(3) Where the head of the plan compilation institution examines a personnel management plan submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and deems supplementation necessary, the head may require the head of the relevant management institution to supplement it. In such cases, the head of the management institution requested to supplement the plan shall supplement and resubmit the personnel management plan within 15 days from the date of receiving the request.
(4) The heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors shall compile the personnel management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and the latter part of paragraph (3) of this Article and submit them to the Minister of the Interior and Safety by November 30 each year pursuant to the latter part of Article 43 (1) of the Act.
(5) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety examines personnel management plans submitted by the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to paragraph (4) and deems supplementation necessary, the Minister may require the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors to supplement them. In such cases, the heads of central administrative agencies and Mayors/Do Governors requested to supplement the plans shall, after hearing the opinions of the heads of the relevant management institutions, supplement and resubmit the personnel management plans within 30 days from the date of receiving the request.

Article 46 (Changes to Personnel Management Plans)
(1) Where the head of a management institution changes a personnel management plan submitted to the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 45 for an unavoidable reason, such as expiry of an agreement with an institution, organization, or corporation to which disaster management personnel subject to management belong (hereinafter referred to as an "institution, etc. to which personnel belong"), the head shall submit the changed personnel management plan (including the reasons for the change) to the Minister of the Interior and Safety through the head of the plan compilation institution without delay.
(2) Article 45 (3) and (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to supplementation of personnel management plans submitted pursuant to paragraph (1).

Article 47 (Matters to Be Recorded and Maintained concerning Disaster Management Personnel)
(1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as contact details and mobilization history of institutions, organizations, and corporations" in Article 43 (3) of the Act means the following matters:
1. Names of institutions, etc. to which personnel belong;
2. Locations and contact details of the head offices of institutions, etc. to which personnel belong (including branch offices, where any);
3. Roles and details of the functions of institutions, etc. to which personnel belong;
4. The number of disaster management personnel capable of being mobilized;
5. Disaster management goods or disaster management property of institutions, etc. to which personnel belong that can be used or utilized when disaster management personnel perform activities;
6. The history of activities of disaster management personnel (including mobilization history);
7. Other matters necessary for management of disaster management personnel prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
(2) The head of a management institution shall assign catalog numbers to institutions, etc. to which personnel belong under that head's jurisdiction in accordance with the standards determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety (hereinafter referred to as the "personnel institution catalog standards").
(3) Article 19 (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to designation and assignment of catalog numbers for institutions, etc. to which personnel belong to which it is difficult to assign catalog numbers in accordance with the personnel institution catalog standards.

CHAPTER VI INTEGRATED MANAGEMENT OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT RESOURCES

Article 48 (Establishment and Operation of Regional Integrated Disaster Management Resource Management Centers)
(1) When establishing and operating a regional integrated disaster management resource management center pursuant to Article 45 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "regional integrated resource management center"), a Mayor/Do Governor shall comply with the guidelines on establishment and operation of regional integrated resource management centers determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
(2) Matters concerning performance on behalf of another of functions related to establishment and operation of regional integrated resource management centers under Article 45 (3) of the Act shall be prescribed by ordinance of the Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province; provided, the costs necessary for such performance on behalf of another shall be determined by the Mayor/Do Governor in accordance with calculation criteria determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.

Article 49 (Establishment and Operation of Integrated Resource Management System)
(1) For systematic and efficient establishment and operation of the integrated resource management system by the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Article 46 (1) of the Act, the head of a management institution shall affix to the relevant disaster management resources barcodes, data patterns, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "barcodes, etc.") assigned by the Minister according to the following catalog numbers:
1. Catalog numbers of disaster management goods under Article 19 (2) and (3);
2. Catalog numbers of disaster management property under Article 44 (2) and (3);
3. Catalog numbers of institutions, etc. to which personnel belong under Article 47 (2) and (3).
(2) Resource management officers, resource receipt and issue officers, and resource utilization officers shall use electronic equipment for reading barcodes, etc. to enter information on the history of management and mobilization of disaster management resources in the integrated resource management system without delay.
(3) The head of a management institution shall designate a person in charge of the integrated resource management system from among public officials, etc. belonging to the institution and require that person to confirm and check the status of entry in the integrated resource management system of information related to disaster management resources under that head's jurisdiction and other relevant matters, thereby ensuring systematic management of information on management and mobilization of disaster management resources under that head's jurisdiction.

Article 50 (Institutions Eligible for Designation as Information Center Operating Institutions)
(1) "Public institution prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 47 (2) 1 of the Act means the Korea Local Information Research and Development Institute under Article 72 of the Electronic Government Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Local Information Research and Development Institute").
(2) "Qualifications prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as capital, equipment, and personnel" in Article 47 (2) 3 of the Act means the following qualifications:
1. Capital of at least 200 million won;
2. Possession of all of the following facilities and equipment:
(a) Facilities and equipment for transmission, receipt, relay, and storage of supply chain management information, disaster management logistics information, and related electronic documents;
(b) Protective facilities and equipment for safe operation of the National Integrated Resource Management Information Center;
(c) Facilities and equipment for management, duplication, and storage of the integrated resource management system;
(d) Facilities and equipment for recording and managing the dates, times, etc. of transmission and receipt of information on disaster management resources and electronic documents stored in the National Integrated Resource Management Information Center;
(e) Facilities and equipment necessary for linkage with information networks related to disaster management resources;
3. Possession of all of the following personnel in accordance with criteria determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety:
(a) A person who holds a national technical qualification as an engineer in information and communications, an engineer in information processing, or an engineer in computer system application, or a higher qualification under the National Technical Qualifications Act, or has at least three years of work experience in a technical field related to development of standard electronic documents for processing, storage, transmission, etc. of information or methods of transmission, receipt, and relay of electronic documents;
(b) A person who holds a certified logistics manager qualification under Article 2 (1) 12 of the Framework Act on Logistics Policies or has at least three years of work experience in the distribution or logistics industry;
(c) A person holding other qualifications that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary for efficient operation of the National Integrated Resource Management Information Center.

Article 51 (Applications for Designation as Information Center Operating Institutions)
(1) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety intends to designate an institution or organization under Article 47 (2) 2 or 3 of the Act as an information center operating institution, the Minister shall determine the application method and other relevant matters in advance and publicly announce them in the Official Gazette or on a website for at least 30 days.
(2) An institution or organization intending to be designated as an information center operating institution in response to a public announcement under paragraph (1) shall submit to the Minister of the Interior and Safety an application for designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, together with the required accompanying documents.
(3) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety receives an application for designation under paragraph (2), the Minister shall comprehensively examine the capability to perform functions and other relevant matters of the institution or organization that applied for designation pursuant to paragraph (2), as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 52 (Issuance of Certificates of Designation as Information Center Operating Institutions)
Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety designates the Local Information Research and Development Institute or an institution or organization that applied for designation pursuant to Article 51 as an information center operating institution, the Minister shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety and publicly announce that fact in the Official Gazette or on a website.

CHAPTER VII MOBILIZATION OF DISASTER MANAGEMENT RESOURCES

Article 53 (Mobilization Orders)
(1) A person issuing a mobilization order pursuant to Articles 50 (1), 51 (1), and 52 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "authority empowered to issue mobilization orders") shall notify the mobilization order in writing (hereinafter referred to as a "written mobilization order"), clearly specifying the following matters, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety:
1. The person receiving the mobilization order;
2. The disaster management resources subject to mobilization;
3. Reasons for mobilization;
4. Date and time of mobilization;
5. Place of mobilization (meaning the location in the case of disaster management resources that cannot be moved);
6. Rules of conduct during mobilization;
7. Other matters that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary in connection with mobilization of disaster management resources.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where an authority empowered to issue mobilization orders deems it necessary for urgent preparedness, response, recovery, etc., the authority may notify a mobilization order by any of the following methods; provided, in the case of subparagraph 3, the written mobilization order shall be delivered after mobilization of the disaster management resources ends:
1. Notification through the integrated resource management system;
2. Notification using an information and communications network under Article 2 (1) 1 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection;
3. Oral notification through an emergency contact network.
(3) A person notified of an order to mobilize disaster management goods and disaster management property (hereinafter referred to as "disaster management goods and property") pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2) shall, for smooth disaster management, provide the disaster management goods and property subject to mobilization (including other disaster management goods and property that can serve as substitutes; hereinafter the same shall apply in this paragraph) at the date, time, and place specified in the mobilization order; provided, where any of the following grounds exists, the person may refrain from providing all or part of the disaster management resource goods and property subject to mobilization:
1. Where compliance with the mobilization order is impossible because business has been suspended or closed due to a disaster, etc., or the disaster management goods and property subject to mobilization have been lost, destroyed, or damaged;
2. Where it is difficult to provide the disaster management goods and property subject to mobilization at the relevant date, time, and place because transport has been paralyzed by a natural disaster, destruction of roads or bridges, or other reasons;
3. Where the owner of the disaster management goods and property subject to mobilization has been declared bankrupt;
4. Where the disaster management goods and property subject to mobilization cannot be provided for other unavoidable reasons.
(4) A person notified of an order to mobilize disaster management personnel pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2) shall, for smooth disaster management, ensure that the disaster management personnel subject to mobilization (including other disaster management personnel who can serve as substitutes; hereinafter the same shall apply in this paragraph) appear at the date, time, and place specified in the mobilization order and comply with official instructions from the head of the relevant management institution; provided, disaster management personnel subject to mobilization need not appear where any of the following grounds exists:
1. Where they are unable to appear due to illness or physical or mental infirmity;
2. Where the house in which they reside has been lost or collapsed due to a disaster, etc., or their household property has suffered a serious disaster, and it would be difficult for anyone other than the person concerned to deal with the situation;
3. Where they are in detention or protective custody or are serving a sentence;
4. Where a lineal ascendant or descendant, spouse, or family member living with them is critically ill or has died, and it would be difficult for anyone other than the person concerned to provide nursing care or conduct the funeral or other arrangements following the death;
5. Where they are traveling abroad;
6. Where there is mobilization training or a call-up for military education under the Military Service Act, or mobilization or training of reserve forces under the Reserve Forces Act;
7. Where they are unable to appear for other unavoidable reasons.
(5) A person who has received a mobilization order under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall enter the status of the mobilized disaster management resources in the integrated resource management system within 30 days after the mobilization ends.

Article 54 (Settlement of Mobilization Costs)
(1) "Criteria prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 53 (2) of the Act means the market price at the time of the mobilization order (where it is difficult to determine the market price, this means the tax base determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety from among tax bases for national or local taxes); provided, where the parties settling costs incurred for mobilization of disaster management resources (hereinafter referred to as "mobilization costs") have agreed on settlement criteria in advance, those criteria shall apply.
(2) Where a budget for settlement of mobilization costs has not been appropriated, mobilization costs may be settled using a fund, reserve fund, etc. falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. The disaster management fund under Article 67 of the Framework Act;
2. The disaster relief fund under Article 14 of the Disaster Relief Act;
3. Reserve funds under Article 22 of the National Finance Act or Article 43 of the Local Finance Act (in the case of management institutions other than administrative agencies, this means budgets appropriated in their own budgets that correspond to reserve funds);
4. Subsidies under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Subsidy Management Act or local subsidies under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Management of Local Government Subsidies;
5. Local allocation tax under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Local Allocation Tax Act.

Article 55 (Mobilization Exercises for Disaster Management Resources)
(1) Exercises concerning mobilization of disaster management resources under Article 55 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "mobilization exercises") shall be conducted as virtual exercises using the integrated resource management system.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), mobilization exercises involving actual mobilization of disaster management resources may be conducted for management institutions.
(3) Where the Minister of the Interior and Safety or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to conduct a mobilization exercise, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall notify persons required to participate in the mobilization exercise under Article 55 (3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "persons subject to mobilization exercises") of the following matters in writing no later than 15 days before the date of the mobilization exercise:
1. Date and time of the mobilization exercise;
2. Place of the mobilization exercise;
3. Content and method of the mobilization exercise;
4. Other matters necessary for the mobilization exercise.
(4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor conducts a mobilization exercise, the Mayor/Do Governor shall submit its results to the Minister of the Interior and Safety within ten days.

CHAPTER VIII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 56 (Surveys of Disaster Management Resources)
(1) The head of a lead disaster management agency under subparagraph 5-2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act (hereinafter referred to in this Article as a "lead disaster management agency") shall, pursuant to Article 56 (1) of the Act, regularly conduct each year a survey of material and human resources that the heads of management institutions need to manage as disaster management goods, disaster management property, or disaster management personnel for disaster management in the field under that agency's jurisdiction (hereinafter referred to as a "resource survey") and inform the Minister of the Interior and Safety of the results in writing by April 30.
(2) Where the head of a lead disaster management agency conducts an ad hoc resource survey, the head shall inform the Minister of the Interior and Safety of the results in writing within ten days.
(3) The Minister of the Interior and Safety may, in consultation with the head of the relevant lead disaster management agency, reflect the results of resource surveys under paragraphs (1) and (2) in the following guidelines:
1. Guidelines for formulation of stockpile management plans;
2. Guidelines for formulation of property management plans;
3. Guidelines for formulation of personnel management plans.

Article 57 (Inspections)
(1) When conducting an inspection concerning management of disaster management resources pursuant to Article 59 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "management inspection"), the head of a management institution shall appoint inspecting public officials, etc. to conduct the inspection.
(2) Management inspections shall be classified into regular inspections and ad hoc inspections.
(3) Regular inspections under paragraph (2) shall be conducted as of the end of each fiscal year.
(4) Ad hoc inspections under paragraph (2) shall be conducted when a resource management officer, resource receipt and issue officer, or resource utilization officer is replaced.
(5) Management inspections shall be conducted by examining disaster management resources and standard forms or by other methods to determine whether acts concerning management of disaster management resources are lawful.
(6) Details necessary for management inspections, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.

Article 58 (Research and Development of National Standards)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety may enter into agreements with institutions, organizations, or business operators falling under any of the subparagraphs of Article 71 (3) of the Framework Act and have them conduct research and development projects concerning the following matters:
1. National standards;
2. Stockpile management standards;
3. Guidelines on maintenance standards under the latter part of Article 29 (2).

Article 59 (Entrustment of Authority)
(1) Pursuant to Article 61 (1) of the Act, the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall entrust the following authority to the heads of relevant central administrative agencies determined and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety; provided, the authority referred to in subparagraphs 2 through 6 shall be limited to national disaster management support enterprises designated pursuant to subparagraph 1:
1. Designation of national disaster management support enterprises under Article 9 (1) of the Act (limited to designation of suppliers of disaster management resources under their jurisdiction);
2. Service of notices stating the duties of national disaster management support enterprises under Article 9 (2) of the Act;
3. Orders for preparatory measures under Article 10 (1) of the Act;
4. Cancellation of designation of national disaster management support enterprises under Article 11 (1) of the Act;
5. Dispatch of documents notifying the fact of cancellation of designation of national disaster management support enterprises under Article 11 (2) of the Act;
6. Imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 65 (3) of the Act.
(2) When making a public notification pursuant to the main clause, other than the subparagraphs, of paragraph (1), the Minister of the Interior and Safety shall include the details of the entrusted functions in the public notification.

Article 60 (Entrustment of Functions)
The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall entrust the following functions to the information center operating institution pursuant to Article 61 (2) of the Act:
1. Surveys of supply chain management information under Article 7 (1) of the Act;
2. Requests for submission of materials necessary for surveys of supply chain management information under Article 7 (2) of the Act;
3. Entry of supply chain management information under Article 8 (1) of the Act;
4. Establishment and operation of the integrated resource management system under Article 46 (1) of the Act;
5. Requests for submission of materials necessary for establishment and operation of the integrated resource management system under Article 46 (2) of the Act;
6. Provision or disclosure of information in the integrated resource management system under Article 46 (3) of the Act.

Article 61 (Processing of Personally Identifiable Information)
(1) Where unavoidable for performing any of the following affairs, the Minister of the Interior and Safety (including persons entrusted with the authority and functions of the Minister of the Interior and Safety pursuant to Articles 59 and 60) may process data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act and alien registration numbers under subparagraph 4 of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "resident registration numbers, etc."):
1. Affairs concerning designation and cancellation of designation of national disaster management support enterprises under Articles 9 and 11 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning establishment and operation of the integrated resource management system under Article 46 of the Act;
3. Affairs concerning designation and revocation of designation of information center operating institutions under Articles 47 and 48 of the Act;
4. Affairs concerning submission of materials, reports, and inspections under Article 59 (1) and (3) of the Act.
(2) Where unavoidable for performing any of the following affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor may process data containing resident registration numbers, etc.:
1. Affairs concerning designation and cancellation of designation of regional disaster management support enterprises under Articles 12 and 14 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning establishment and operation of regional integrated resource management centers under Article 45 of the Act;
3. Affairs concerning submission of materials, reports, and inspections under Article 59 (2) and (3) of the Act.
(3) Where unavoidable for performing any of the following affairs, the head of a management institution may process data containing resident registration numbers, etc.:
1. Affairs concerning management of disaster management personnel under Article 42 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning mobilization of disaster management resources and measures for compliance with mobilization orders under Article 49 of the Act.
(4) Where unavoidable for performing affairs concerning mobilization orders, an authority empowered to issue mobilization orders may process data containing resident registration numbers, etc.

